description:
collectCleavages() merges cleavage events with the same (top, bottom) on its last line: `events.filter((e, i) => i === 0 || e.top !== events[i-1].top || e.bottom !== events[i-1].bottom)`. It keeps whichever event sorts first by (lo, hi, top, enzyme name, siteStart, strand). Only afterwards does digestLinear() run its per-site validity check: `e.siteStart < dsStart || e.siteStart + e.siteSize > dsEnd`, i.e. the recognition site must be fully double-stranded. Validity therefore depends on the site, but merging ignores it.

When two different footprints make the same double-strand break and the one that sorts first overlaps a single-stranded input end, the merged event is skipped with reason 'end'. The valid event was already discarded, so the cut silently disappears. Consequences:
- adding an enzyme to a digest can REMOVE a cut that another enzyme makes on its own;
- the lost enzyme is not listed in `skipped`;
- the one skip reason reported ("recognition site is not fully double-stranded") is false for the site that should have cut.

This also breaks the module's own invariant that every cleavage event is either applied or reported.

Scope: linear digests with sticky `options.ends` only. With blunt ends, and on circles, validity depends only on (top, bottom), so merging is harmless there.

Coincident breaks are not exotic:
- every BsaI or BsmBI site contains a BsmAI site making the same break;
- Type IIS sites are often placed to recreate a restriction overhang (BsmBI + EcoRI, AATT);
- inverted MlyI sites 15 bp apart cut at the same gap;
- BaeI reports forward and reverse matches 3 bp apart with identical events.

An independent first-principles reference, fuzzed over 6,000 linear digests with sticky ends (overhangs up to 10 nt, Type IIS sites near the ends), disagreed in 7 cases, all with this root cause (BsmBI alone, EcoRI+BsmBI, BaeI combinations). The implementer's oracles miss it because sticky-end cases keep sites at least 45 bp (live oracle) or 50 bp (static oracle) from the ends.

Fix: apply the linear 'end' and recognition-site checks per event BEFORE merging identical breaks, or, when merging, keep a representative whose site is valid.

